When a source’s error listener sets the media element’s src, Ladybird
now abandons the old selection, and loads only that src. Run on the new
test, Chrome 157.0.8103.79 and Firefox 151.0 both do the same.

When the listener instead adopts the element into a document that isn’t
fully active, Ladybird now waits until the element is back in a fully
active document, then goes on to the next source, which loads. Both
engines load that source once the element is back, too. Firefox goes on
asynchronously: If the element comes back from the next task, it does
just what Ladybird does, but after 100ms, it has already fired error at
the next source in the inactive document, and loads it again once the
element is back. Chrome fires that error too, but then loads the source,
since its HTMLMediaElement::AttachToNewFrame() restarts the load on
every adoption into another document.
